WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.541
Code review for upload features should focus on the full workflow.

2
00:00:04.541 --> 00:00:19.057
Review upload endpoints, request limits, file name handling, path construction, storage locations, validation decisions, scanning and processing states, download handlers, cleanup jobs, and administrative tools.

3
00:00:19.057 --> 00:00:23.873
A safe endpoint is not enough if the storage or download path is weak.

4
00:00:23.873 --> 00:00:25.799
Dependencies deserve review.

5
00:00:25.799 --> 00:00:40.522
Upload middleware, image libraries, document libraries, archive libraries, CSV parsers, storage SDKs, scanning integrations, preview services, and conversion tools influence how files are interpreted and processed.

6
00:00:40.522 --> 00:00:49.329
Teams should know which libraries are maintained, how they are configured, what limits are set, and how updates will be applied.

7
00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:52.975
Storage configuration is part of deployment security.

8
00:00:52.975 --> 00:01:03.432
Local storage, network shares, cloud object storage, temporary directories, and container volumes each have different permission and lifecycle concerns.

9
00:01:03.432 --> 00:01:10.174
Review who can read, write, delete, list, publish, back up, and restore files in each environment.

10
00:01:10.174 --> 00:01:16.848
Protect storage credentials, connection strings, API keys, service identities, and CI/CD secrets.

11
00:01:16.848 --> 00:01:25.792
Upload pipelines often involve the web app, processing workers, scanning services, object storage, queues, and deployment systems.

12
00:01:25.792 --> 00:01:34.185
Each component should have only the access it needs, and credentials should be managed through approved secret mechanisms.

13
00:01:34.185 --> 00:01:38.038
Environment-specific upload settings should be explicit.

14
00:01:38.038 --> 00:01:41.959
Development may use small local stores and test scanners.

15
00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:44.987
Staging may mimic production with test data.

16
00:01:44.987 --> 00:01:52.142
Production needs controlled limits, monitoring, retention, storage policies, and restricted diagnostics.

17
00:01:52.142 --> 00:01:57.645
A development shortcut should not become a production upload policy by accident.

18
00:01:57.645 --> 00:02:01.911
Legacy remediation should be prioritized by risk and exposure.

19
00:02:01.911 --> 00:02:16.427
Inventory upload paths, identify public and administrative entry points, locate storage roots, review download handlers, add limits, isolate storage, improve authorization checks, and document approved patterns.

20
00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:23.101
The goal is steady movement toward a workflow that future developers can understand and maintain.
